  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "04r8qeCrKqSzPXw59ySZ+AHcOGE=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:13.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`dispatch` has three parameters, not two:\n\n```ts\ndispatch(\n  actionOrActions: TAction | TAction[] | undefined,\n  onErrors?: (errors: Error[]) => void,\n  onSuccess?: (result: PHDocument) => void,\n): void\n```\n\n`onErrors` surfaces per-action reducer failures; `onSuccess` hands you the updated document the moment the operation lands, which is what you want when the next UI step depends on server-assigned data (a new revision number, a header field) rather than on the action input you already have.\n\nThe implementation is `dispatchActions` in `packages/reactor-browser/src/actions/dispatch.ts`, and the ordering there matters. It resolves the document, signs the actions, queues them, and only then runs the callbacks:\n\n```ts\nconst result = await queueActions(document, signedActionsWithContext);\n\nif (onErrors && result) {\n  const errors = getActionErrors(result, signedActionsWithContext);\n  if (errors.length) onErrors(errors);\n}\nif (onSuccess && result) onSuccess(result);\n```\n\nBoth callbacks are guarded on `result`, so **neither fires** when the dispatch bailed out early \u2014 an unresolvable document id or a failure to build signed actions logs an error and returns `undefined` without notifying either callback. `onSuccess` is also not exclusive of `onErrors`: `result` is the document after the operations were appended, and a reducer that threw still produces an operation carrying `.error`. So a dispatch whose actions all failed validation can invoke `onErrors` **and then** `onSuccess` with the (state-unchanged) document. Treat `onSuccess` as \"the write round-tripped\", not as \"the write was accepted\".\n\nDocumentation caveat: `04-Reference/05-EditorsUI/01-ReactHooks.md` still prints the two-argument signature everywhere, including in the \"Dispatch Function\" key-concepts section and in every hook's return type. The three-argument form is documented only in the learn-path page and is confirmed by source.\n\n_Source: packages/reactor-browser/src/actions/dispatch.ts and src/hooks/dispatch.ts, plus apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/04-editors/03-editor-hooks-and-state.mdx (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:13.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:13.000Z"
    }
  },
